\title{Tie Geometry of Arithmetic Statistics:\\
Exact Resolution Ceilings for the 2-adic Dial\\
and its Quadratic-Residue Baselines}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{3 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop an exact, parameter-free calculus for the maximal rank correlation attainable
by a discrete statistic, and apply it to adjudicate an empirical comparison in a
cryptographic measurement pipeline. The central object is the \emph{tie profile} of a
statistic---the multiset of level-set cardinalities---and the central functional is its
\emph{cube sum} $\sum_j m_j^3$. We prove that the Kendall tie correction to Spearman's
$\rho$ equals $\tfrac{1}{12}\bigl(\sum_j m_j^3 - n\bigr)$, whence the attainable squared
correlation ceiling is $1 - (\sum_j m_j^3 - n)/(n^3-n)$, strictly decreasing in the cube
sum at fixed sample size $n$; coarsening a statistic increases the cube sum and therefore
lowers the ceiling.

Two exact ceilings follow. The trailing-zero (2-adic valuation) statistic at bit-length
$b$, with $N=2^b$, has ceiling $\tfrac{6}{7}\bigl(1 + \tfrac{1}{N(N+1)}\bigr)$. The bare
quadratic-residue indicator modulo an odd prime has ceiling \emph{exactly} $3/4$, for
every odd prime---a prime-independence law obtained from the vanishing of the quadratic
character sum, which forces the residue classes to split as $(m,m+1)$ modulo $p=2m+1$.

The comparison this enables is sharp. A recorded measurement at bit-length $48$ on
uniform draws reports Spearman correlations $0.777/0.755/0.801$ across three independent
seeds, all inside a validation band $[0.55,0.85]$, exceeding the bare quadratic-residue
baseline by $+0.09$ to $+0.13$ on every seed. We prove a \emph{gap law}: the entire
tie-geometry advantage of the 2-adic dial over the bare quadratic-residue count is
$\sqrt{6/7} - \sqrt{3/4} < 0.06$ in correlation units, at every odd prime and every
bit-length. Consequently the recorded advantage cannot be a resolution artefact, and
forces the baseline reading at least $0.03$ below its own ceiling. We further establish
a multiplicative tower law for joint Legendre vectors over several primes, a counting
collapse theorem, the resulting crossover hierarchy at bit-length~$48$, a
replicated-symbol tower with closed form $1-(9^r-3^r)/(27^r-3^r)\ge 1-2\cdot 3^{-r}$, and
a band-saturation asymmetry showing that a validation band calibrated on one statistic is
not transportable to the other.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Spearman rank correlation; tie correction; 2-adic valuation;
Legendre symbol; quadratic residues; quadratic character sums; Chinese Remainder Theorem;
resolution ceilings.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

Consider a cryptographic sampling pipeline in which each drawn integer $x$ is assigned a
downstream scalar outcome, a \emph{rate}. A candidate predictor---henceforth the
\emph{dial}---is the trailing-zero count
\[
  T(x) \;=\; v_2(x) \;=\; \max\{\,k : 2^k \mid x\,\},
\]
the 2-adic valuation, computable in a single machine instruction on standard hardware.

The empirical record under study concerns uniform draws at bit-length $48$. Across three
independent seeds the Spearman rank correlation between $T$ and the rate reads
\[
  \rho_1 = 0.777, \qquad \rho_2 = 0.755, \qquad \rho_3 = 0.801,
\]
all inside the pre-registered validation band $[0.55,0.85]$, with a seed spread of
$0.046 < 0.05$. On the same draws the dial exceeds a \emph{bare quadratic-residue count}
baseline by between $+0.09$ and $+0.13$ on every seed.

\subsection{The confound}

The comparison is not of like with like. The dial is many-valued with geometrically
decaying level sets; the quadratic-residue indicator is two-valued with two nearly equal
level sets. Spearman's $\rho$ computed against a statistic with heavy ties is
mechanically attenuated: ties compress the midrank vector, reducing its variance and
hence the attainable correlation with \emph{any} target. The question is therefore: how
much of the recorded $0.09$--$0.13$ advantage is attributable to tie granularity alone?
The answer below is exact, requires no simulation, and needs no distributional assumption
about the rate.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item A \emph{cube-sum calculus} (\S\ref{sec:cube}): the tie correction of a profile
  equals $\tfrac{1}{12}(\sum_j m_j^3 - n)$; the ceiling is
  $1 - (\sum_j m_j^3 - n)/(n^3-n)$; and at fixed $n$ the ceiling is strictly antitone in
  the cube sum.
\item An \emph{arithmetic bridge} (\S\ref{sec:profiles}): a character-sum proof that
  modulo $p=2m+1$ there are exactly $m+1$ squares and $m$ non-squares.
\item The \emph{prime-independence law} (\S\ref{sec:prime}): the bare quadratic-residue
  ceiling is exactly $3/4$ for every odd prime.
\item The \emph{multiplicative tower law} (\S\ref{sec:tower}) and the \emph{counting
  collapse} (\S\ref{sec:collapse}).
\item The \emph{crossover hierarchy} and \emph{replicated-symbol tower}
  (\S\ref{sec:crossover}).
\item The \emph{gap law} and its slack corollary (\S\ref{sec:gap}), plus
  \emph{band-saturation asymmetry} and \emph{envelope flatness} (\S\ref{sec:calib}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setting and definitions}

\begin{definition}[Tie profile]
Let $S$ be a statistic on a finite population $\Omega$ with $|\Omega| = n$. The
\emph{tie profile} of $S$ is the list $L = (m_1,\dots,m_k)$ of cardinalities of the
level sets $S^{-1}(v)$, $v \in S(\Omega)$. Necessarily $\Sigma L := \sum_j m_j = n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cube sum]
For a profile $L=(m_1,\dots,m_k)$ set $\cube(L) := \sum_{j=1}^k m_j^3$.
\end{definition}

The cube sum is additive over concatenation and satisfies $\cube(aL)=a^3\cube(L)$ under
entrywise multiplication.

\begin{definition}[Kendall tie correction]
For a profile $L$ set $\mathcal{T}(L) := \sum_j \frac{m_j^3-m_j}{12}$.
\end{definition}

This is the classical correction term in the tie-adjusted Spearman statistic: when a
statistic with profile $L$ is rank-transformed using midranks, the variance of the
resulting rank vector falls from its untied value $\frac{n^3-n}{12}$ to
$\frac{n^3-n}{12}-\mathcal{T}(L)$.

\begin{definition}[Resolution ceiling]
For a profile $L$ with $n=\Sigma L \ge 2$ set
\[
  \ceil(L) := 1 - \frac{12\,\mathcal{T}(L)}{n^3-n}, \qquad \rmax(L) := \sqrt{\ceil(L)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
By Cauchy--Schwarz applied to the midrank vector of $S$ against the rank vector of any
target, the squared Spearman correlation of $S$ with that target is at most the ratio of
tied to untied midrank variance, which is exactly $\ceil(L)$. The bound is attained when
the target is strictly monotone in $S$ and untied within blocks, so $\ceil$ is the exact
supremum. If all $m_j=1$ then $\ceil(L)=1$; if $k=1$ then $\ceil(L)=0$.
\end{remark}

\section{The cube-sum calculus}\label{sec:cube}

\begin{theorem}[Tie correction in cube-sum form]\label{thm:tiecube}
For every profile $L$, $\;12\,\mathcal{T}(L) = \cube(L) - \Sigma L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induct on the length of $L$. For $L=()$ both sides vanish. For $L=(m)\frown L'$, the
correction splits as $\frac{m^3-m}{12}+\mathcal{T}(L')$, while $\cube$ and $\Sigma$ split
as $m^3+\cube(L')$ and $m+\Sigma L'$. Apply the induction hypothesis and add.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tie-attenuation law]\label{thm:atten}
For every profile $L$ with $n=\Sigma L \ge 2$,
\[
  \ceil(L) \;=\; 1 - \frac{\cube(L)-n}{n^3-n}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:tiecube} into the definition of $\ceil$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:atten} isolates the design principle: at fixed sample size the ceiling
depends on the statistic \emph{only} through its cube sum. All semantic content of the
statistic enters through this one scalar.

\begin{theorem}[Cube-sum monotonicity]\label{thm:anti}
Let $L,M$ be profiles with $\Sigma L=\Sigma M=n\ge 2$ and $\cube(L)\le\cube(M)$. Then
$\ceil(M)\le\ceil(L)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:atten}, $\ceil(L)-\ceil(M) = \bigl(\cube(M)-\cube(L)\bigr)/(n^3-n)$,
and $n^3-n>0$ for $n\ge 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Coarsening lowers the ceiling]\label{cor:coarse}
If $M$ arises from $L$ by replacing two entries $a,b$ with the single entry $a+b$, then
$\ceil(M)\le\ceil(L)$, strictly if $a,b\ge 1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$\Sigma$ is unchanged and $(a+b)^3 = a^3+b^3+3ab(a+b) \ge a^3+b^3$, with equality only if
$ab(a+b)=0$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:anti}.
\end{proof}

Informally: a statistic can never be improved, in the resolution sense, by forgetting how
it distinguishes two of its values.

\section{The two profiles}\label{sec:profiles}

\subsection{The dyadic profile}

\begin{definition}
For $b\ge 1$ the \emph{dyadic profile} at bit-length $b$ is
$D_b = \bigl(2^{b-1},2^{b-2},\dots,2,1,1\bigr)$, the tie profile of $T=v_2$ on the
residues modulo $N=2^b$: exactly $N/2^{k+1}$ have $v_2=k$ for $0\le k\le b-1$, and one
(namely $0$) has valuation $\ge b$. Thus $\Sigma D_b = N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Dyadic ceiling]\label{prop:dyadic}
With $N=2^b$,
\[
  \ceil(D_b) \;=\; \frac{6}{7}\left(1+\frac{1}{N(N+1)}\right).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The cube sum is $\sum_{k=0}^{b-1} 8^{\,b-1-k} + 1 = \frac{N^3-1}{7}+1 = \frac{N^3+6}{7}$.
By Theorem~\ref{thm:atten},
\[
  \ceil(D_b) = 1 - \frac{N^3-7N+6}{7(N^3-N)}
  = 1 - \frac{(N-1)(N-2)(N+3)}{7N(N-1)(N+1)}
  = 1 - \frac{(N-2)(N+3)}{7N(N+1)}.
\]
Since $7N(N+1)-(N^2+N-6)=6(N^2+N+1)$, this equals
$\frac{6(N^2+N+1)}{7N(N+1)} = \frac67\bigl(1+\frac{1}{N(N+1)}\bigr)$.
\end{proof}

Consequently $\ceil(D_b)>6/7$ for every $b$, $\ceil(D_b)$ is strictly decreasing in $b$,
and $\ceil(D_b)-6/7 < 4^{-b}$. At $b=48$,
\[
  \ceil(D_{48}) \in \bigl(6/7,\ 6/7+10^{-14}\bigr), \qquad \rmax(D_{48}) = 0.9258200997\ldots
\]
The constant $6/7$ is the fixed point of the halving cascade: a geometric profile with
ratio $1/2$ has cube sum $\frac{1}{1-1/8}$ times its largest cube, i.e.\ $\frac17$ of
$N^3$, destroying one seventh of the available resolution.

\subsection{The quadratic-residue profile}

\begin{definition}
For $m\ge 1$ set $Q_m := (m,\ m+1)$, with $\Sigma Q_m = 2m+1$ and
$\cube(Q_m) = m^3+(m+1)^3$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Arithmetic bridge]\label{thm:bridge}
Let $p$ be an odd prime. Then the number of squares in $\Zmod{p}$ (with $0$ counted as a
square) exceeds the number of non-squares by exactly one. Writing $p=2m+1$: there are
$m+1$ squares and $m$ non-squares, so the tie profile of the quadratic-residue indicator
is $Q_m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\chi$ be the quadratic character of $\Zmod{p}$, so $\chi(0)=0$, $\chi(a)=1$ for $a$
a nonzero square and $\chi(a)=-1$ for $a$ a non-square; this trichotomy uses that the
characteristic is not $2$. Split the full character sum along the square/non-square
partition:
\[
  \sum_{a\ \mathrm{square}} \chi(a) \;+\; \sum_{a\ \mathrm{non\text{-}square}} \chi(a)
  \;=\; \sum_{a\in\Zmod{p}} \chi(a) \;=\; 0,
\]
the vanishing being orthogonality of the nontrivial character $\chi$ against the trivial
one. The second sum equals $-\#\{\text{non-squares}\}$. The first equals
$\#\{\text{squares}\}-1$, since $0$ is a square contributing $0$ rather than $+1$ while
every other square contributes $+1$. Hence
$\#\{\text{squares}\}-1-\#\{\text{non-squares}\}=0$; as the classes partition a set of
size $2m+1$, the cardinalities are $m+1$ and $m$.
\end{proof}

\section{The prime-independence law}\label{sec:prime}

\begin{theorem}[Prime independence]\label{thm:prime}
For every $m\ge 1$, $\;\ceil(Q_m) = \tfrac34$ exactly, hence
$\rmax(Q_m)=\sqrt3/2 = 0.8660254\ldots$ for \emph{every} odd prime modulus $p=2m+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The key identity is the factorisation
\[
  m^3+(m+1)^3 \;=\; (2m+1)(m^2+m+1),
\]
verified by expanding the right side to $2m^3+3m^2+3m+1 = m^3+(m^3+3m^2+3m+1)$. Hence
with $n=2m+1$,
\[
  \cube(Q_m)-n = (2m+1)(m^2+m+1)-(2m+1) = (2m+1)\,m(m+1),
\]
while
\[
  n^3-n = (2m+1)\bigl((2m+1)^2-1\bigr) = (2m+1)\cdot 4m(m+1).
\]
The two share the nonzero factor $(2m+1)m(m+1)$, so their quotient is exactly $\tfrac14$
with the modulus cancelling identically. By Theorem~\ref{thm:atten},
$\ceil(Q_m)=1-\tfrac14=\tfrac34$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
For a two-block profile $(\alpha n,(1-\alpha)n)$ the ceiling tends to
$1-(\alpha^3+(1-\alpha)^3)=3/4$ at $\alpha=1/2$. The Legendre split is
$\alpha = m/(2m+1)$, off balance by exactly one element; the factorisation above shows the
finite-$m$ correction vanishes identically rather than merely being $O(m^{-2})$. This
exact cancellation is special to the split $(m,m+1)$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:below}
For all $m,b\ge 1$: $\;\ceil(Q_m)=\tfrac34 < \tfrac67 < \ceil(D_b)$. The bare
quadratic-residue count has strictly lower resolution than the trailing-zero dial at every
bit-length and every odd prime modulus.
\end{corollary}

\section{The multiplicative tower law}\label{sec:tower}

By the Chinese Remainder Theorem the joint statistic
$x\mapsto(\chi_{p_1}(x),\dots,\chi_{p_r}(x))$ over distinct odd primes has level sets
indexed by the product of the individual level-set families, with cardinalities the
products of the individual block sizes.

\begin{definition}[Product profile]
For profiles $A,B$ set $A\otimes B := (ab)_{a\in A,\,b\in B}$, with $|A|\cdot|B|$ entries.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:prod}
$\Sigma(A\otimes B) = (\Sigma A)(\Sigma B)$ and $\cube(A\otimes B)=\cube(A)\cube(B)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Distribute over $A$ and use $\Sigma(aB)=a\Sigma B$ and $\cube(aB)=a^3\cube(B)$, both
immediate inductions on $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Legendre vector profile]
For $m_1,\dots,m_r$ with each $p_i=2m_i+1$ prime, set
$V(m_1,\dots,m_r) := Q_{m_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes Q_{m_r}$, the tie profile of the joint
Legendre vector on a full residue system modulo $N=\prod_i p_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative tower law]\label{thm:tower}
With $N=\prod_i(2m_i+1)\ge 2$ and $C=\prod_i\bigl(m_i^3+(m_i+1)^3\bigr)$,
\[
  \ceil\bigl(V(m_1,\dots,m_r)\bigr) \;=\; 1-\frac{C-N}{N^3-N}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:prod} and induction, $\Sigma V = N$ and $\cube(V)=C$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:atten}.
\end{proof}

Both constituents are multiplicative across CRT factors, so the ceiling factorises with
no interaction term. Using Theorem~\ref{thm:prime}'s identity,
$C = \prod_i p_i \cdot \prod_i (m_i^2+m_i+1)$, so
\[
  \frac{C}{N^3} \;=\; \prod_i \frac{m_i^2+m_i+1}{(2m_i+1)^2} \;=\; \prod_i \frac{p_i^2+3}{4p_i^2},
\]
a clean per-prime factor: $1/3$ at $p=3$, $7/25$ at $p=5$, $13/49$ at $p=7$, decreasing to
$1/4$ as $p\to\infty$.

\begin{example}
For primes $3$ and $5$ ($m=1,2$): $V=(2,3,4,6)$, $N=15$, $C=315$, so
$\ceil(V) = 1-\frac{300}{3360} = \frac{51}{56} = 0.910714\ldots$
\end{example}

\section{The counting collapse}\label{sec:collapse}

A practitioner combining $r$ Legendre symbols is likely to \emph{sum} them: record how
many of the $r$ tests report ``square''. This coarsens the vector statistic, and
Corollary~\ref{cor:coarse} predicts a loss.

\begin{definition}[Convolution step]
For $a,b\in\mathbb{N}$ and a profile $L=(c_0,c_1,\dots)$ indexed by count value, let
$\mathrm{mB}_{a,b}(L)$ be the coefficient list of $(b+az)\sum_i c_i z^i$. Concretely
$\mathrm{mB}_{a,b}(())=()$ and
$\mathrm{mB}_{a,b}((c)\frown L) = (bc)\frown \mathrm{addHead}\bigl(ac,\mathrm{mB}_{a,b}(L)\bigr)$,
where $\mathrm{addHead}(x,\cdot)$ adds $x$ to the leading entry (creating it if the list
is empty).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Legendre count profile]
$K(m_1,\dots,m_r)$ is the coefficient list of $\prod_i\bigl((m_i+1)+m_i z\bigr)$; its
$j$-th entry counts residues modulo $N$ at which exactly $j$ of the $r$ symbols report
``non-square''.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Ksum}
$\Sigma K(m_1,\dots,m_r) = \prod_i(2m_i+1) = N = \Sigma V(m_1,\dots,m_r)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Evaluate the generating polynomial at $z=1$: each factor contributes $(m_i+1)+m_i=2m_i+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cube superadditivity of merging]\label{lem:merge}
For $x\in\mathbb{N}$ and any profile $L$,
$x^3+\cube(L)\le\cube\bigl(\mathrm{addHead}(x,L)\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $L$ is empty both sides agree. Otherwise $L=(y)\frown t$ and the right side is
$(x+y)^3+\cube(t) = x^3+y^3+3xy(x+y)+\cube(t)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Convolution step is cube-superadditive]\label{lem:conv}
For all $a,b$ and every profile $L$,
$(a^3+b^3)\cube(L)\le\cube\bigl(\mathrm{mB}_{a,b}(L)\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $L$; the empty case is trivial. For $L=(c)\frown L'$,
\[
  \cube\bigl(\mathrm{mB}_{a,b}(L)\bigr)
  = (bc)^3 + \cube\bigl(\mathrm{addHead}(ac,\mathrm{mB}_{a,b}(L'))\bigr)
  \ \ge\ b^3c^3 + a^3c^3 + \cube\bigl(\mathrm{mB}_{a,b}(L')\bigr)
\]
by Lemma~\ref{lem:merge}, and the induction hypothesis bounds the last term below by
$(a^3+b^3)\cube(L')$. Summing gives
$(a^3+b^3)\bigl(c^3+\cube(L')\bigr) = (a^3+b^3)\cube(L)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:cubecmp}
$\cube\bigl(V(m_1,\dots,m_r)\bigr)\le\cube\bigl(K(m_1,\dots,m_r)\bigr)$ for every list.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $r$; for $r=0$ both equal $1$. For the step, by Lemma~\ref{lem:prod},
$\cube(V(m\frown\mathbf{m}')) = (m^3+(m+1)^3)\cube(V(\mathbf{m}'))$, which by the
induction hypothesis is at most $(m^3+(m+1)^3)\cube(K(\mathbf{m}'))$, which by
Lemma~\ref{lem:conv} with $a=m$, $b=m+1$ is at most $\cube(K(m\frown\mathbf{m}'))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Counting collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
For every list of odd primes $p_i=2m_i+1$ with $N=\prod_i p_i \ge 2$,
\[
  \ceil\bigl(K(m_1,\dots,m_r)\bigr) \;\le\; \ceil\bigl(V(m_1,\dots,m_r)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equal sample sizes by Lemma~\ref{lem:Ksum}; larger cube sum by
Proposition~\ref{prop:cubecmp}; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:anti}.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Strictness at two primes]
For $p=3,5$: $K(1,2)=(6,7,2)$, $\cube = 567$, $N=15$, so
$\ceil(K(1,2)) = 1-\frac{552}{3360} = \frac{117}{140} = 0.835714\ldots < \frac{51}{56}$.
Summing the two symbols costs $0.075$ of squared ceiling.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Three primes, counted]
For $p=3,5,7$: $K(1,2,3)=(24,46,29,6)$, $N=105$, $\cube=135765$, so
$\ceil(K(1,2,3)) = 1-\frac{135660}{1157520} = \frac{2433}{2756} = 0.882801\ldots$
\end{example}

\section{Crossover hierarchy and the replicated tower}\label{sec:crossover}

\begin{theorem}[Crossover hierarchy at bit-length 48]\label{thm:cross}
With $\ceil(D_{48})\in(6/7,\ 6/7+10^{-14})$,
\[
  \ceil(Q_1)=\tfrac34 \;<\; \ceil(K(1,2))=\tfrac{117}{140} \;<\; \ceil(D_{48})
  \;<\; \ceil(K(1,2,3))=\tfrac{2433}{2756} \;<\; \ceil(V(1,2))=\tfrac{51}{56}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Numerically $0.7500 < 0.8357 < 0.857142857\ldots < 0.8828 < 0.9107$, and the bracketing of
$\ceil(D_{48})$ from Proposition~\ref{prop:dyadic} separates the middle comparisons with
margin exceeding $10^{-2}$.
\end{proof}

Thus three Legendre symbols are required before a quadratic-residue \emph{count} can, on
tie geometry alone, out-resolve the trailing-zero dial at bit-length $48$; but only two
suffice if kept as a \emph{vector}. The counting collapse is exactly what shifts the
crossover from two symbols to three.

\begin{theorem}[Replicated-symbol tower]\label{thm:repl}
Let $r\ge 1$ and take $r$ independent Legendre symbols at the prime $3$, kept as a vector.
Then
\[
  \ceil\bigl(V(\underbrace{1,\dots,1}_{r})\bigr)
  \;=\; 1-\frac{9^r-3^r}{27^r-3^r} \;\ge\; 1-\frac{2}{3^{\,r}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:prod}, $N=3^r$ and $C=(1^3+2^3)^r=9^r$; Theorem~\ref{thm:tower} with
$N^3=27^r$ gives the closed form. For the bound put $x=3^r\ge 3$; then
\[
  \frac{2}{x}-\frac{x^2-x}{x^3-x}
  = \frac{2(x^3-x)-x(x^2-x)}{x(x^3-x)}
  = \frac{x^3+x^2-2x}{x(x^3-x)}
  = \frac{x^2+x-2}{x^3-x} \;\ge\; 0
\]
for $x\ge 1$, using $9^r=x^2$ and $27^r=x^3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Two Legendre symbols at the prime $3$, kept as a vector, already attain ceiling
$1-\frac{72}{720}=\frac{9}{10}$, strictly above $\ceil(D_{48})$.
\end{corollary}

Convergence is geometric: $r$ symbols reach within $2\cdot 3^{-r}$ of perfect resolution.
Resolution is bought by recording more, not by choosing better primes.

\section{The gap law}\label{sec:gap}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:num}
$\rmax(D_{48}) < 0.9259$ and $\rmax(Q_m) > 0.866$ for every $m\ge 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\ceil(D_{48}) < 6/7+10^{-14} < 0.9259^2$ and $\ceil(Q_m)=3/4 > 0.866^2$; take square
roots, monotone on $[0,\infty)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gap law]\label{thm:gap}
For every $m\ge 1$, $\;\rmax(D_{48}) - \rmax(Q_m) < 0.06$. The exact value is
$\sqrt{6/7+\varepsilon}-\sqrt{3/4} = 0.0597947\ldots$ with $\varepsilon<10^{-28}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Proposition~\ref{prop:num}: $0.9259-0.866 = 0.0599 < 0.06$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The recorded gap forces slack]\label{thm:slack}
Let $t$ be the dial's recorded correlation and $q$ the baseline's, on the same sample at
bit-length $48$ with odd prime modulus $p=2m+1$. If $t\le\rmax(D_{48})$ and
$t-q\ge 0.09$, then $q \le \rmax(Q_m) - 0.03$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$q \le t-0.09 \le \rmax(D_{48})-0.09 < \rmax(Q_m)+0.06-0.09 = \rmax(Q_m)-0.03$, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:gap}.
\end{proof}

Tie granularity can account for at most $0.06$ of the recorded $0.09$--$0.13$
advantage---at most two thirds of the smallest recorded gap and under half of the largest.
The residual is not geometry: the baseline reading must sit at least $0.03$ below its own
attainable maximum.

\begin{proposition}[Readings are admissible]
With the recorded top reading $t=0.801$ and advantage in $[0.09,0.13]$, every compatible
baseline value $q\in[0.671,0.711]$ satisfies $q^2 \le 0.5055 < 3/4 = \ceil(Q_m)$; and
every recorded dial value satisfies $t^2 \le 0.6416 < 6/7 < \ceil(D_{48})$. Both readings
are consistent with their ceilings, so the analysis is not vacuous.
\end{proposition}

\section{Calibration consequences}\label{sec:calib}

\begin{theorem}[Band-saturation asymmetry]\label{thm:band}
Let $B=0.85$ be the upper edge of the validation band $[0.55,0.85]$. Then for every
$m\ge 1$,
\[
  \rmax(Q_m)-B < 0.017, \qquad \rmax(D_{48})-B > 0.07 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\rmax(Q_m)=\sqrt3/2 < 0.8661$, so the first difference is below $0.0161$; and
$\rmax(D_{48}) > \sqrt{6/7} > 0.9258$, so the second exceeds $0.0758$.
\end{proof}

The band leaves the baseline under $2\%$ of headroom and the dial over $7.5\%$. A
validation band calibrated on one statistic is not transportable to a statistic with a
different ceiling; fixed acceptance intervals impose unequal standards across estimators
of unequal intrinsic resolution. The remedy is to report ceiling-normalised correlations
$\rho/\rmax$, which for the record here read $0.839$, $0.815$, $0.865$.

\begin{theorem}[Envelope flatness]\label{thm:flat}
Across the recorded deployment envelope $b\in[44,52]$ the dyadic ceiling is strictly
decreasing in $b$, yet $\ceil(D_{44})-\ceil(D_{52}) < 2^{-80}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Strict antitonicity follows from Proposition~\ref{prop:dyadic} since $N(N+1)$ is strictly
increasing. For the bound, $\ceil(D_{44})-6/7 < 4^{-44}=2^{-88}$ and $\ceil(D_{52})>6/7$.
\end{proof}

Hence the empirically observed bit-length dependence of the dial across bit-lengths
$44$--$52$ cannot be tie geometry, which moves by less than $10^{-24}$.

\section{Algorithms}

All quantities above are exactly computable in rational arithmetic.

\paragraph{Algorithm A (Ceiling of a profile).} Input a profile $L$; compute
$n=\Sigma L$ and $c=\cube(L)$; return $1-(c-n)/(n^3-n)$ as an exact rational. Cost $O(k)$
big-integer operations for $k$ blocks.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (Legendre vector and count profiles).} Given primes $p_i=2m_i+1$,
build the vector profile by iterated product ($2^r$ entries, cost $O(2^r)$) and the count
profile by convolving $(m_i+1)+m_i z$ ($r+1$ entries, cost $O(r^2)$). Feed both to
Algorithm A. The count profile is exponentially cheaper and provably no better.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (Gap audit).} Given $b$, an odd prime $p$, a recorded dial
correlation $t$ and baseline correlation $q$: compute $\rmax(D_b)$ and
$\rmax(Q_{(p-1)/2})=\sqrt3/2$; report the geometric budget $G=\rmax(D_b)-\rmax(Q_m)$ and
the forced slack $S=(t-q)-G$. If $S>0$ the advantage exceeds anything tie geometry can
produce. At $b=48$: $G=0.05979$, so an advantage of $0.09$ forces $S\ge 0.0302$, and an
advantage of $0.13$ forces $S\ge 0.0702$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What is and is not established}
The results concern \emph{resolution}, not the mechanism linking $v_2$ to the downstream
rate. We prove the recorded advantage cannot be explained by tie granularity; we do not
identify what does explain it. Conversely the negative results are unconditional: the
bare quadratic-residue ceiling of exactly $3/4$ holds for every odd prime with no error
term, and $3/4<6/7$ at every bit-length; the counting collapse holds for every finite list
of primes.

\subsection{Two-block rigidity}
The ceiling of a two-valued statistic is a pure function of its class-mass split, so all
arithmetic content of the indicator---reciprocity, character-sum estimates,
equidistribution---enters only through a single scalar bias. No theorem about the
pseudo-randomness of Legendre symbols can raise the ceiling of a single Legendre symbol
above $3/4$.

\subsection{Vector versus count: a design rule}
When combining several binary arithmetic probes into a composite baseline, retain the
joint vector rather than the count whenever downstream cost permits. At two primes the
difference is $0.9107$ versus $0.8357$ in squared units. The count is exponentially
cheaper to store; the price is a strictly lower ceiling, quantified by
Theorem~\ref{thm:collapse}.

\subsection{Reporting recommendation}
Raw correlation bands are the wrong unit for cross-statistic comparison. Report
$\rho/\rmax$, or report $\rmax$ alongside $\rho$, making the geometric budget explicit and
preventing the silent double standard a fixed band imposes.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Sharp two-block cliff for arithmetic indicators.} Conjecture: every balanced
  arithmetic indicator---one whose two classes differ in size by at most one on a full
  residue system modulo $n$---has ceiling in $[3/4,\ 3/4+3/(4n^2)]$, with equality $3/4$
  exactly on the odd-prime Legendre indicator; and no arithmetic indicator with $k$ classes
  can exceed $1-1/k^2$ on a modulus supporting equidistribution.
\item \textbf{Multiplicative ceiling spectrum.} For squarefree $M=p_1\cdots p_r$ the
  joint-Legendre ceiling is
  $1-\bigl(\prod_i \frac{p_i^2+3}{4p_i^2}M^3 - M\bigr)/(M^3-M)$, so the achievable ceilings
  form the multiplicative semigroup generated by $\{(p^2+3)/(4p^2)\}$. Conjecture: this
  semigroup is dense in $(0,1)$, so for every target $t\in(3/4,1)$ some squarefree modulus
  has Legendre-vector ceiling within $10^{-6}$ of $t$.
\item \textbf{Capped dials.} Truncating $T$ at a cap merges the tail blocks; the resulting
  ceiling follows from Proposition~\ref{prop:dyadic} by a single merge, and the register
  width / resolution trade-off can be optimised exactly.
\item \textbf{Mixed towers.} Combining a dyadic dial with a Legendre vector gives a product
  profile whose ceiling follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:prod}; the cheapest composite statistic
  reaching a target ceiling is a discrete optimisation with multiplicative cost.
\item \textbf{Beyond Spearman.} Kendall's $\tau_b$ and the Goodman--Kruskal $\gamma$ carry
  their own tie corrections; whether an analogous exact prime-independence law holds for
  them is open.
\end{enumerate}

